WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.280
A protein made in a nerve cell's body may have to reach a synapse a metre away.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.980
Left to diffuse, it would take centuries, so how does it get there?

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.820
The cell hands it to a motor, a protein that walks.

4
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:20.653
Its road is a microtubule, and the road has a direction.

5
00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:25.893
In an axon, nearly every microtubule points its plus end out.

6
00:00:27.273 --> 00:00:29.013
Kinesin-1 walks toward the plus end.

7
00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:32.613
Dynein walks toward the minus end, back home.

8
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:37.113
Watch one foot. It grips the track.

9
00:00:38.293 --> 00:00:41.253
It swings while gripping, which pulls the motor forward.

10
00:00:42.573 --> 00:00:43.953
Then it lets go and re-cocks.

11
00:00:45.273 --> 00:00:48.013
Each turn of this cycle is driven by one ATP.

12
00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:54.537
So a motor has feet on a polar track, each repeating grip, swing, let go, re-cock.

13
00:00:55.877 --> 00:01:00.197
Which way the swing points helps set which way a motor goes, though dynein is less

14
00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:01.197
tidy.

15
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:03.337
Kinesin-1 has two feet.

16
00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:05.717
The back foot lets go.

17
00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:11.417
The front foot, still gripping, swings and tips it past, onto the next site.

18
00:01:12.737 --> 00:01:13.877
Then the feet swap roles.

19
00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:19.077
Think of walking: your hips move one step, your back foot twice as far.

20
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:23.870
The cargo rides at the hips, moving 8 nanometres per step.

21
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.530
Each foot moves about 16.

22
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.530
And at low load, each step costs one ATP.

23
00:01:31.783 --> 00:01:33.003
Here is the trick.

24
00:01:34.323 --> 00:01:38.423
The gripping foot nearly always stays on until the stepping foot lands.

25
00:01:39.643 --> 00:01:43.363
So one kinesin can take about 100 steps before it lets go.

26
00:01:44.543 --> 00:01:47.823
A motor that keeps walking like this is called processive.

27
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:53.667
Now a test. Muscle myosin II also has two feet.

28
00:01:55.027 --> 00:01:57.987
Could one molecule of it carry a vesicle all on its own?

29
00:01:59.427 --> 00:02:00.427
Pause and predict.

30
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:11.490
No. Each myosin II foot spends most of its cycle off the track.

31
00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.330
The two feet do not take turns.

32
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.310
One molecule soon falls away.

33
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.390
So myosin II works in crowds, hundreds of heads per thick filament.

34
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.250
That crowd shortens muscle.

35
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.830
Heads grip the thin actin filaments, swing, and let go.

36
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.170
The thin filaments slide inward between the thick ones.

37
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.750
No filament gets shorter.

38
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.330
They just overlap more.

39
00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:41.453
Back to the metre.

40
00:02:43.073 --> 00:02:47.473
Cargo often carries several motors, so while one lets go, another still holds.

41
00:02:48.753 --> 00:02:51.793
How much this matters in cells is still being worked out.

42
00:02:52.783 --> 00:02:55.763
So a motor walks by repeating one small cycle.

43
00:02:57.023 --> 00:03:00.223
Keep a foot on the track, and one molecule can travel far.

44
00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:04.043
Let go too often, and only a crowd can pull.
